// The three durations typing is made of, in milliseconds.
//
// NAMES.md section 5: a family of constants is `<prefix>_base() + n`, with one
// bare literal in the family, because two zero-action functions returning the
// same literal anywhere in the program are a duplicate-logic error and "pick
// numbers that happen not to clash" is not a strategy across a dozen modules.
// 400 is this family's, and it is registered there. The base itself, tin_base,
// is a constant in engine/conf.id.

// How long a key must be held before it starts repeating. Short enough that
// holding Backspace to clear a field is a reflex rather than a wait, long enough
// that a deliberate single tap never doubles: the range everything agrees on is
// 400-600 ms, and X11's own default is 660.
tin_delay() {
  int ret_i = (import tin_base) + 20;
} return int ret_i;

// And how often it repeats after that: 30 ms, so 33 characters a second. A
// frame at 60 fps is 16 ms, so this fires on every second frame at most -- which
// matters, because tin_press fires a key at most once per call and a rate faster
// than the frame would silently become the frame rate.
tin_rate() {
  int ret_i = (import tin_base) - 370;
} return int ret_i;
